• Emergency Escharotomy Indicated for circumferential full thickness extremity burns with compromised distal pulses or torso burns restricting respiratory excursions Involves longitudinal incision through the rigid eschar into subcutaneous fat • Early Tangential Excision Performed within Days 2 5 post stabilization horizontal shaving of necrotic eschar down to healthy punctate bleeding tissue followed by immediate split thickness skin grafting STSG --- **2 Reconstructive Surgery and The Reconstructive Ladder** **Definition** Reconstructive surgery is a surgical discipline focused on restoring normal anatomical form function and aesthetic contour to tissues deformed or lost secondary to trauma congenital anomalies oncologic resection or burn sequelae **The Reconstructive Ladder vs Reconstructive Elevator** RECONSTRUCTIVE LADDER Level 6 Free Tissue Transfer Microvascular Level 5 Distant / Regional Pedicled Flaps Level 4 Local Tissue Flaps Z plasty Adv Level 3 Full Thickness Skin Grafts FTSG Level 2 Split Thickness Skin Grafts STSG Level 1 Secondary Intention / Primary Closure RECONSTRUCTIVE ELEVATOR Choose the technique providing the BEST functional and aesthetic result not necessarily the simplest e g exposed bare bone / tendon requires an immediate flap **Surgical Flap Classification** • By Blood Supply • Random Pattern Flaps Supplied by non directional microvascular subdermal plexus strict length to width ratio 1 1 to 1 5 1 • Axial Pattern Flaps Supplied by a well defined named anatomical arteriovenous system running along the long axis e g Groin flap Deltopectoral flap • Perforator Flaps Supplied by isolated perforating vessels traversing deep fascia / muscle allowing flap harvest while preserving underlying muscle e g DIEP ALT • By Tissue Composition Cutaneous Fasciocutaneous skin and fat and deep fascia Myocutaneous muscle and skin paddle and Osteocutaneous bone and soft tissue • By Flap Movement Advancement V Y rectangular Transposition Limberg rhomboid Z plasty Rotation semicircular arc and Interpolation pedicle crosses intact skin e g Paramedian Forehead flap **Mathes and Nahai Classification of Muscle Flaps** • Type I Single vascular pedicle One single artery / vein Minor Secondary None Clinical Examples Tensor Fasciae Latae TFL Gastrocnemius • Type II Dominant and Minor pedicles Dominant supplies muscle Minor enters distal base Clinical Examples Gracilis Trapezius Soleus • Type III Two codominant pedicles Both major either sustains Minor Secondary None Clinical Examples Rectus Abdominis Gluteus Maximus • Type IV Segmental pedicles No single dominant Multiple along length Clinical Examples Sartorius Tibialis Anterior • Type V 1 Dominant and Segmentals One large dominant supply Segmentals support base Clinical Examples Latissimus Dorsi Pectoralis Major --- **3 Skin Grafts** **Definition** A skin graft is a segment of avascular completely detached epidermis and dermis harvested from a donor site and transferred to a recipient vascular bed relying entirely on host bed neovascularization for survival **Classification STSG vs FTSG** **Split Thickness Skin Graft STSG** • Composition Epidermis and Variable portion of Dermis 0 008 0 018 in • Donor Site Healing Re epithelializes spontaneously from adnexa follicles • Primary Contraction Minimal approximately 10 percent immediate elastic recoil • Secondary Contraction High myofibroblast contracture of recipient bed • Graft Take / Survival Rapid highly reliable tolerates less vascular beds • Aesthetic Match Inferior shiny pigmentary changes contour dip • Primary Indications Extensive burns large traumatic wounds fasciotomies **Full Thickness Skin Graft FTSG** • Composition Epidermis and Entire Dermis greater than 0 020 in • Donor Site Healing Requires primary surgical closure or local flap • Primary Contraction High approximately 40 50 percent recoil upon harvest • Secondary Contraction Minimal thick dermis resists wound bed contracture • Graft Take / Survival Slower requires pristine highly vascularized bed • Aesthetic Match Superior matches color texture and hair • Primary Indications Facial defects eyelids hands aesthetic units **Stages of Skin Graft Healing Graft Take** 1 Plasmatic Imbibition Hours 0 48 The avascular graft acts like a sponge passively absorbing nutrients electrolytes and oxygen from host wound exudate by capillary action fibrin acts as temporary anchor Graft weight increases by 30 40 percent 2 Inosculation Days 2 4 Direct end to end alignment and micro anastomoses kissing vessels establish connection between graft microvessels and budding host capillaries 3 Revascularization and Angiogenesis Day 4 onward Host endothelial capillary ingrowth penetrates the graft dermis establishing mature arterial inflow venous outflow and lymphatic drainage Fibroblasts deposit permanent anchoring collagen **Causes of Graft Failure** 1 Hematoma / Seroma Most common cause fluid elevates graft from the bed disrupting imbibition and inosculation 2 Shearing Forces Motion severs fragile microvessels 3 Infection Bioburden greater than 10 5 organisms / g or Group A Streptococcus secretes streptokinase dissolving the fibrin bond 4 Inadequate Bed Exposed avascular bone lacking periosteum tendon without paratenon or cartilage without perichondrium --- **4 Cosmetic Aesthetic Surgery** **Definition** Cosmetic surgery is an elective subdiscipline of plastic surgery directed toward enhancing restoring and harmonizing normal anatomical structures to optimize physical appearance facial balance and patient self perception **Core Aesthetic Surgical Procedures** • 1 Rhytidectomy Facelift • Anatomy Focuses on repositioning the SMAS Superficial Musculoaponeurotic System a fibromuscular layer continuous with platysma and temporoparietal fascia • Techniques SMAS plication / imbrication suturing / resecting SMAS redundancy vs Deep Plane Facelift dissecting beneath SMAS releasing zygomatic and masseteric retaining ligaments to suspend midface and jowls without skin tension • 2 Blepharoplasty Eyelid Rejuvenation • Upper Blepharoplasty Excision of redundant upper lid skin dermatochalasis and conservative resection of herniated medial / central fat compartments • Lower Blepharoplasty Subciliary or transconjunctival approach addresses lower lid steatoblepharon bags via fat resection or fat transposition across the tear trough arcus marginalis release • 3 Rhinoplasty • Approaches Closed endonasal incisions vs Open trans columellar inverted V incision providing wide structural exposure • Modifications Dorsal hump resection osteotomies to close open roof deformity spreader grafts to maintain internal nasal valve angle greater than 10 15 degrees and columellar strut grafts for tip support • 4 Abdominoplasty Tummy Tuck • Indicated for postpartum or massive weight loss abdominal wall laxity • Operative Steps Low transverse suprapubic incision Elevation of dermoadipose flap to costal margins Plication of rectus abdominis anterior fascia corrects rectus diastasis Transposition of umbilicus Resection of redundant skin apron • 5 Liposuction Suction Assisted SAL Ultrasound Assisted UAL or Power Assisted PAL Involves subcutaneous infiltration of Kleins Tumescent Solution Lactated Ringers and dilute Epinephrine 1 1 000 000 and Lignocaine to induce vasoconstriction minimizing blood loss and providing regional anesthesia --- **5 Scar Management and Contracture Correction** **Definition** Scar Management encompasses prophylactic and therapeutic strategies to optimize wound remodeling Contracture is the pathological rigid shortening of mature scar tissue across joints or orifices resulting in functional limitation and anatomical distortion **Hypertrophic Scar vs Keloid** **Hypertrophic Scar** • Growth Extent Confined strictly within original wound margins • Etiology and Genetics Related to wound tension infection or delayed healing • Onset and Course Develops early within 4 weeks often regresses over 1 2 yrs • Common Sites Flexor surfaces joints areas under mechanical tension • Histology Organized parallel bundles of Type III collagen • Surgical Recurrence Low recurrence after tension free revision **Keloid** • Growth Extent Invades beyond original wound boundaries into normal skin • Etiology and Genetics Strong genetic predisposition common in darker skin Fitzpatrick IV VI • Onset and Course Develops late months / years after minor trauma progressive growth • Common Sites Sternal presternal region earlobes deltoid upper back jawline • Histology Disorganized thick whorled glassy Type I and III collagen • Surgical Recurrence Very high recurrence 45 100 percent if excised without adjuvants **Therapeutic Scar Management Spectrum** • Non Surgical Measures • Silicone Gel Sheeting First line standard increases stratum corneum hydration and downregulates cytokine mediated fibroblast hyperactivity • Pressure Therapy Custom elastic garments applying 15 25 mmHg continuous pressure for 23 hours / day • Intralesional Injections Triamcinolone Acetonide 10 40 mg / mL and 5 Fluorouracil 5 FU at 4 6 week intervals inhibits fibroblast proliferation and collagen synthesis • Laser Therapy 595 nm Pulsed Dye Laser PDL to ablate microvascular feeding vessels **Surgical Correction of Contractures** • Z Plasty Principles and Angles A double transposition flap technique that lengthens a contracted scar along its central limb and reorients scar direction into relaxed skin tension lines RSTL • 30 30 degrees Z plasty 25 percent theoretical gain in length • 45 45 degrees Z plasty 50 percent theoretical gain in length • 60 60 degrees Z plasty Classic Standard 75 percent theoretical gain in length with a 90 degrees change in scar axis • W Plasty and Geometric Broken Line Closure Breaks up long linear facial scars into irregular zig zag interdigitations to diffuse light and camouflage scars into RSTLs without changing length --- **6 Microvascular Surgery and Free Tissue Transfer** **Definition** Microvascular surgery is a specialized operative discipline utilizing high power optical magnification operating microscopes specialized micro instruments and micro sutures 8 0 to 11 0 to perform vascular anastomoses on small vessels 0 5 3 0 mm and coaptation of peripheral nerve fascicles **Surgical Technique Microvascular Anastomosis** 1 Vessel Preparation Mobilize donor and recipient vessels under the microscope circumferentially strip perivascular adventitia back 2 3 mm from cut edges to prevent thrombogenic adventitial prolapse into the lumen 2 Mechanical Dilation and Flushing Gently dilate vessel ends with jewelers forceps irrigate lumen with heparinized saline 100 IU / mL to clear debris and microthrombi 3 Bi triangulation / Triangulation Place two stay sutures at 180 degrees or three at 120 degrees using 9 0 or 10 0 nylon Keep stay sutures under gentle traction to keep anterior and posterior vessel walls separated 4 Suture Placement Place interrupted through and through sutures across adventitia media and intima ensuring precise intima to intima eversion without catching the opposite back wall 5 Reperfusion Release the venous clamp first followed by the arterial inflow clamp Confirm patency using the Acland micro forceps strip test **Common Workhorse Free Flaps** **Anterolateral Thigh ALT** • Tissue Harvested Fasciocutaneous / Myocutaneous • Vascular Pedicle Descending branch of Lateral Circumflex Femoral Artery • Major Clinical Applications Head and neck reconstruction oral cavity soft tissue defects **Free Fibula Flap** • Tissue Harvested Osteocutaneous Vascularized bone • Vascular Pedicle Peroneal Artery and Venae Comitantes • Major Clinical Applications Mandibular / Maxillary reconstruction long bone defects **Radial Forearm RFFF** • Tissue Harvested Thin pliable Fasciocutaneous • Vascular Pedicle Radial Artery and Venae Comitantes / Cephalic vein • Major Clinical Applications Intraoral lining tongue floor of mouth pharynx **DIEP Flap** • Tissue Harvested Skin and Subcutaneous Fat Spares muscle • Vascular Pedicle Deep Inferior Epigastric Perforators • Major Clinical Applications Autologous post mastectomy breast reconstruction **Latissimus Dorsi LD** • Tissue Harvested Muscle / Myocutaneous • Vascular Pedicle Thoracodorsal Artery and Vein • Major Clinical Applications Large soft tissue coverage chest wall scalp defects **Postoperative Monitoring and Flap Salvage** • Clinical Parameters Checked hourly for first 24 hrs • Color and CRT Healthy pink with 1 2 sec refill vs Pale arterial thrombosis vs Violaceous / Congested with less than 1 sec rapid refill venous thrombosis • Temperature Monitored via surface probe drop greater than 2 3 degrees C indicates perfusion compromise • Pinprick Bleeding Bright red within 1 2 sec is normal absent bleeding indicates arterial occlusion dark purple rapid bleeding indicates venous outflow obstruction • Emergency Flap Salvage Immediate return to theatre within 4 6 hours of suspected compromise Re exploration involves evacuating compressive hematomas topical vasodilators Papaverine catheter directed thrombolysis tPA re doing anastomoses or interposition vein grafting","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm focusing intently on tool selection, understanding their diverse applications. It's clear I must prioritize the most efficient tool for any given task, be it specific file viewing or broader command execution. I am currently evaluating the most useful tools.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Choices**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on precise tool utilization. Remembering the core rules, I'm avoiding `cat` for file operations and generic commands where specific tools are available. I've begun to consider the complete set of applicable tools before execution. My next task requires extracting and formatting content from a PDF; I am identifying the best combination of tools for this task.\n\n\n**Analyzing User Intent**\n\nI've hit a snag. The user's request appears straightforward, \"just the question answers,\" but the PDF doesn't explicit