{"step_index":5,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T13:57:48Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nCARDIAC SURGERIES Easy-language study notes | Based on the supplied Cardiac Surgeries / Bailey & Love pages 823–849 These notes simplify the supplied source for exam revision. Where the requested heading (especially contraindications, heart transplant, or physiotherapy) is not specifically detailed in the supplied pages, it is clearly treated as a general study addition rather than presented as source text. 1. OPEN-HEART SURGERY Introduction / Definition Open-heart surgery is an operation in which the chest is opened so the surgeon can directly operate on the heart or great vessels. The heart is commonly approached through a median sternotomy. Types • On-pump open-heart surgery – uses cardiopulmonary bypass (CPB). • Off-pump surgery – selected operations, such as some CABG procedures, are performed on the beating heart. Indications • CABG for suitable coronary artery disease. • Congenital heart defects requiring repair or palliation. • Aortic aneurysm/dissection surgery. • Selected valve and other cardiac operations. Contraindications / limitations The supplied chapter does not give a single list of absolute contraindications. In practice, surgery is limited when operative risk is greater than expected benefit, anatomy is unsuitable, or severe irreversible comorbidity makes surgery inappropriate. Procedure • Anaesthesia and monitoring. • Median sternotomy and opening of the pericardium. • Heparinisation if CPB is required. • CPB cannulation and establishment of bypass. • Myocardial protection and the planned repair. • Rewarming, restoration of coronary flow and weaning from CPB. • Reverse anticoagulation and close the chest. The source describes median sternotomy, full heparinisation before CPB, and alternative arterial/venous cannulation sites. filecite turn10file5 L334-L359 Complications • Bleeding/coagulopathy. • Arrhythmias and low cardiac output. • Respiratory complications. • Neurological dysfunction/stroke. • Infection. • Renal or other organ dysfunction. Management Pre-op: assessment, investigations, optimise comorbidities, medication review and patient education. Medical: cardiac medicines as prescribed, fluid/electrolyte control, analgesia, anticoagulation/antiplatelet management and antibiotics when indicated. Post-op: ICU monitoring, respiratory support, pain and wound care, haemodynamic monitoring and early mobilisation. PT: positioning, breathing exercises, supported cough, secretion clearance when indicated, limb exercises and progressive mobility. Page 1 Cardiac Surgeries | Easy-language notes2. HEART-LUNG BYPASS / CARDIOPULMONARY BYPASS (CPB) Definition CPB is a machine-based system that temporarily takes over circulation and ventilation, allowing the heart to be isolated for surgery. Indications / uses • Major open-heart operations. • Complex congenital and valve surgery. • CABG when an on-pump technique is selected. • Selected aortic operations. • Other uses described by the source include severe respiratory failure, pulmonary embolectomy, lung transplantation and cardiopulmonary trauma. filecite turn10file3 L184-L203 Circuit / Procedure Venous blood → venous reservoir → oxygenator → pump → arterial circulation. • Arterial cannula is commonly placed in the ascending aorta; femoral or axillary sites may be used when needed. • Venous drainage is commonly through the right atrium/vena cavae. • The pump takes over circulation; the oxygenator performs gas exchange. • A heat exchanger can cool or rewarm blood and reduce metabolic demand. • A filter removes particulate emboli before blood returns to the arterial circulation. Source description of the circuit and cannulation: filecite turn10file3 L208-L228 Myocardial protection • Cardioplegia. • Intermittent cross-clamp fibrillation. • Total circulatory arrest for selected complex procedures. The source notes that permanent myocardial damage can develop within about 15–20 minutes without protection. filecite turn10file4 L304-L318 Coming off CPB • Remove the aortic cross-clamp and restore coronary perfusion. • Cardiovert if ventricular fibrillation persists. • Correct temperature, acidosis and hypokalaemia. • Restart ventilation and gradually reduce pump flow. • When cardiac function and blood pressure are adequate, discontinue CPB, remove cannulae and reverse heparin with protamine. Source: filecite turn10file0 L29-L40 Complications • Bleeding/coagulopathy, infection, air embolism and microembolisation. • Vascular injury and myocardial depression. • Neurological dysfunction. • Pulmonary injury and systemic organ dysfunction. • Post-CPB inflammatory response may progress to multiorgan failure. The source summary lists bleeding disorders, infection, air embolism, intestinal ischaemia, microembolisation, myocardial depression, neurological dysfunction, pancreatitis, pulmonary injury, vascular injury and systemic organ dysfunction. filecite turn11file5 L299-L312 Management Pre-op: assess cardiac, respiratory, renal and coagulation status. Medical: anticoagulation during CPB and correction of temperature, acid-base and electrolytes. Post-op: ICU monitoring for bleeding, cardiac output, respiratory and neurological Page 2 Cardiac Surgeries | Easy-language notesproblems. PT: respiratory assessment, lung expansion, secretion manage